title: Sending a signal one way or the other
mode: vhdl
chapter: Choosing
check: behaves
---- intro
A multiplexer picks one of many inputs. A **demultiplexer** does the opposite: it takes one input `d` and sends it to one of two outputs, chosen by `s`. The other output stays `'0'`.

| s | y0 | y1 |
|---|----|----|
| 0 | `d` | 0 |
| 1 | 0 | `d` |

Each output is its own `when ... else` line.
---- task
Send `d` to `y0` when `s` is 0, and to `y1` when `s` is 1. The other output is `'0'`.
---- start
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity demux is
  port(
    d  : in  std_logic;
    s  : in  std_logic;
    y0 : out std_logic;
    y1 : out std_logic
  );
end demux;

architecture rtl of demux is
begin
    -- write it here
end rtl;
---- answer
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity demux is
  port(
    d  : in  std_logic;
    s  : in  std_logic;
    y0 : out std_logic;
    y1 : out std_logic
  );
end demux;

architecture rtl of demux is
begin
    y0 <= d when s = '0' else '0';
    y1 <= d when s = '1' else '0';
end rtl;
---- hint1
One line for each output, each `... <= d when s = ... else '0';`
---- hint2
`y0 <= d when s = '0' else '0';` and `y1 <= d when s = '1' else '0';`
---- solution
`y0 <= d when s = '0' else '0';` and `y1 <= d when s = '1' else '0';` Only one output can let `d` through at a time.
---- done
Multiplexers gather signals in, demultiplexers send them out. Memory chips use both to read and write one cell out of millions.
